---

<Docs
  title="Protocol v1"
  description={`The ${site.name} add-on protocol, version ${PROTOCOL_VERSION}: routes, objects and fields.`}
  toc={toc}
>
  <h1>Protocol v{PROTOCOL_VERSION}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    The reference for what an add-on answers. The <a href="/docs/sdk/">SDK</a> implements all of it;
    you only need this page to check a detail or to write an add-on in another language. For whole
    objects to copy, see the <a href="/docs/data-model/">data model samples</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="routes">Routes</h2>
  <p>
    All paths are relative to the add-on’s base URL: the manifest URL without{' '}
    <code>manifest.json</code>. An add-on can live under a path, such as{' '}
    <code>https://catalog.{site.domain}/librivox/manifest.json</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={routes} />
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>{'{type}'}</code> is a content type: <code>audiobook</code>, <code>podcast</code> or{' '}
      <code>episode</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>{'{id}'}</code> is percent-encoded with <code>encodeURIComponent</code>, so{' '}
      <code>example:secret-garden</code> arrives as <code>example%3Asecret-garden</code> and a{' '}
      <code>/</code> in an ID as <code>%2F</code>. Split the path on <code>/</code> first, then
      decode each segment.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>{'{extra}'}</code> is a query string in a path segment, such as{' '}
      <code>genre=children&amp;skip=20</code>. The app sorts the keys alphabetically, leaves out
      empty values and encodes keys and values with <code>encodeURIComponent</code>, so the same
      request always has the same URL and caches well. Decode <code>+</code> as a space, as in a
      query string. Without extras the path is{' '}
      <code>
        /catalog/{'{type}'}/{'{id}'}.json
      </code>
      .
    </li>
  </ul>
  <CodeBlock code={`${catalogUrl}\n${searchUrl}\n${metaUrl}\n${streamUrl}`} label="Examples" />

  <h2 id="http">HTTP</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Answer <code>GET</code> (and <code>HEAD</code>). Answer <code>OPTIONS</code> preflights with{' '}
      <code>204</code> and the CORS headers.
    </li>
    <li>
      Send <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *</code> on every response, errors included. The web
      app calls add-ons straight from the browser.
    </li>
    <li>
      Respond with <code>Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8</code>.
    </li>
    <li>Serve over HTTPS: the web app can’t reach plain HTTP from an HTTPS page.</li>
    <li>
      Answer within 10 seconds. The app gives up on slower answers and shows what other add-ons
      returned.
    </li>
    <li>
      Catalog, meta and stream responses may carry <code>cacheMaxAge</code>: how many seconds the
      response may be kept. Send the same value as <code>Cache-Control: public, max-age=…</code>
      so HTTP caches agree; the SDK does both, and gives the manifest an hour.
    </li>
    <li>
      Times and lengths are integer milliseconds (<code>durationMs</code>, <code>startMs</code>),
      sizes are bytes, <code>released</code> is ISO 8601 and <code>cacheMaxAge</code> is seconds.
    </li>
    <li>
      Use only the fields on this page. The app drops fields it doesn’t know, and the{' '}
      <a href="#schema">JSON Schema</a> rejects them.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <CodeBlock code={headers} label="Response headers" />

  <h2 id="ids">IDs and routing</h2>
  <p>
    Every item has an ID of the form <code>prefix:value</code>. The prefix starts with a lowercase
    letter and has only lowercase letters, digits, <code>_</code> and <code>-</code>; the value is
    anything that isn’t empty. The whole ID is 3 to 512 characters.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Prefix</th>
          <th scope="col">Value</th>
          <th scope="col">Example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code>ol:</code>
          </td>
          <td>An Open Library work ID. Canonical for books.</td>
          <td>
            <code>ol:OL69612W</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code>librivox:</code>
          </td>
          <td>The archive.org identifier of a LibriVox recording.</td>
          <td>
            <code>librivox:secret_garden_librivox</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code>asin:</code>
          </td>
          <td>An Audible ASIN. The Open Library catalog add-on answers its meta from Audnexus.</td>
          <td>
            <code>asin:B002UZMLXM</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code>podcast:</code>
          </td>
          <td>A podcast’s GUID.</td>
          <td>
            <code>podcast:917393e3-1b1e-5cef-ace4-edaa54e1f810</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Use an <code>ol:</code> ID for a book when you know it, or name it in <code>externalIds</code>.
    An <code>asin</code> in <code>externalIds</code> ties a book to its Audible recording, whose
    narrators, series and chapters the Open Library catalog add-on reads from Audnexus. For your own
    items, pick a prefix of your own.
  </p>
  <p>
    The app decides whom to ask for an item’s <code>meta</code> and <code>stream</code>:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      The add-on must list the resource in <code>resources</code>, and the item’s type in that
      resource’s <code>types</code> (or, without them, in the manifest’s <code>types</code>).
    </li>
    <li>
      The ID must start with one of the resource’s <code>idPrefixes</code>. A resource object’s{' '}
      <code>idPrefixes</code> replace the manifest’s; without either, the add-on is asked for any
      ID. Include the colon (<code>ol:</code>, not <code>ol</code>), since the app compares the
      start of the ID.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>meta</code> comes from the first matching add-on, in the order the listener keeps them.{' '}
      <code>stream</code> is asked of every matching add-on, and the answers are merged in that
      order.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    In the example manifest below, <code>meta</code> is only asked for <code>example:</code> IDs,
    but <code>stream</code> also for <code>ol:</code> IDs: the add-on offers its recordings for any
    Open Library book the listener opens, whichever add-on listed it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="manifest">Manifest</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GET /manifest.json</code> answers with the manifest itself.
  </p>
  <FieldTable fields={manifestFields} />
  <h3 id="resource">Resource</h3>
  <p>
    A <code>resources</code> entry is a name (<code>"catalog"</code>, <code>"meta"</code>,{' '}
    <code>"stream"</code>, <code>"scrobble"</code>) or an object:
  </p>
  <FieldTable fields={resourceFields} />
  <CodeBlock code={manifest} label="GET /manifest.json" />

  <h2 id="catalogs">Catalogs</h2>
  <p>
    A catalog is a list of items to browse or search. It answers with{' '}
    <code>{'{ "metas": MetaPreview[], "cacheMaxAge"?: number }'}</code>. Only catalogs declared in
    the manifest are requested.
  </p>
  <h3 id="catalog-definition">CatalogDefinition</h3>
  <FieldTable fields={catalogFields} />
  <h3 id="extras">CatalogExtra</h3>
  <FieldTable fields={extraFields} />
  <p>The extras the app understands:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>search</code>: what the listener typed. A catalog with a <code>search</code> extra can
      be searched; when it’s required, the catalog only answers searches and stays off Home.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>genre</code>: one of the <code>options</code>, shown as filters.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>skip</code>: how many items to skip, as a decimal string, for the next page. Return an
      empty <code>metas</code> list when there are no more.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Catalogs without a required extra can appear on Home.</p>
  <h3 id="meta-preview">MetaPreview</h3>
  <p>One item in a catalog: enough to draw a cover and a title.</p>
  <FieldTable fields={previewFields} />
  <CodeBlock code={catalog} label={`GET ${catalogUrl}`} />

  <h2 id="meta">Meta</h2>
  <p>
    <code>
      GET /meta/{'{type}'}/{'{id}'}.json
    </code>{' '}
    answers with <code>{'{ "meta": Meta, "cacheMaxAge"?: number }'}</code>, or a <code>404</code>{' '}
    when the ID is unknown. Meta has every MetaPreview field, plus:
  </p>
  <FieldTable fields={metaFields} />
  <h3 id="chapter">Chapter</h3>
  <FieldTable fields={chapterFields} />
  <h3 id="series">Series</h3>
  <FieldTable fields={seriesFields} />
  <h3 id="episode">Episode</h3>
  <FieldTable fields={episodeFields} />
  <h3 id="edition">Edition</h3>
  <p>
    Since revision 9. The item page lists a work’s editions when there are two or more; the listener
    can choose one, and the page then shows its cover, year, narrators and length, and asks add-ons
    for audio by its <code>externalIds</code> as well as the work’s. The choice stays on the device.
    The Open Library catalog lists a work’s audio editions.
  </p>
  <FieldTable fields={editionFields} />
  <CodeBlock code={meta} label={`GET ${metaUrl}`} />
  <CodeBlock code={podcast} label={`GET ${podcastUrl}`} />

  <h2 id="streams">Streams</h2>
  <p>
    <code>
      GET /stream/{'{type}'}/{'{id}'}.json
    </code>{' '}
    answers with <code>{'{ "streams": Stream[], "cacheMaxAge"?: number }'}</code>. Each stream is
    one way to play the item, such as one reading of a book. With nothing to offer, answer{' '}
    <code>{'{ "streams": [] }'}</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A stream is either a single file, with <code>url</code>, or a list of files, with{' '}
    <code>parts</code>; never both. Both kinds have these fields:
  </p>
  <FieldTable fields={streamFields} />
  <h3 id="single-file">Single file</h3>
  <FieldTable fields={singleFields} />
  <h3 id="parts">Parts</h3>
  <FieldTable fields={multiFields} />
  <h3 id="stream-part">StreamPart</h3>
  <FieldTable fields={partFields} />
  <p>
    With parts, each part becomes a chapter. A single file uses its own <code>chapters</code>, or
    the meta’s.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={streams} label={`GET ${streamUrl}`} />
  <h3 id="resolve">Links that expire</h3>
  <p>
    If your source hands out audio URLs that stop working after a while, give the stream or part a
    stable <code>url</code> on your add-on instead, such as <code>/resolve/…</code>, and answer it
    with a <code>302</code> redirect to a fresh audio URL and <code>Cache-Control: no-store</code>.
    When the audio host later answers <code>403</code> or <code>410</code>, the player and the
    downloader request your <code>url</code> again, once per expiry, and continue at the same place.
    The web player can’t see the status of a failed audio request, so it does this after any network
    error. The audio still goes straight from the source to the listener.
  </p>

  <h2 id="errors">Errors</h2>
  <p>
    Answer errors with a non-2xx status and <code>{'{ "error": string }'}</code>: <code>404</code>
    for an unknown route, catalog or ID, <code>400</code> for extras you can’t use, <code>5xx</code>
    when something upstream failed. The app treats a failing add-on as having nothing right now and
    keeps the answers of the others.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={errorExample} />

  <h2 id="scrobble">Scrobble</h2>
  <p>
    An add-on with <code>scrobble</code> in <code>resources</code> hears what the listener plays,
    for example to keep a reading tracker or another podcast app up to date. The app sends{' '}
    <code>POST /scrobble</code> with a JSON body of up to 100 events, oldest first, to every
    installed add-on that declares it for the item’s type. A Resource object’s{' '}
    <code>idPrefixes</code> narrow it to those IDs; the manifest’s own prefixes don’t apply here.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>start</code> when playing starts, <code>progress</code> about every five minutes while
      it plays, <code>pause</code> when it stops or another item takes over, and <code>finish</code>{' '}
      at the end, or when the listener marks a book as finished. <code>abandon</code> when the
      listener chooses Stop reading in a book’s menu. Since revision 10, <code>rate</code> when the
      listener rates a book on its page or in its menu, and a <code>rate</code> without{' '}
      <code>rating</code> when they remove the rating.
    </li>
    <li>
      Since revision 10, a Resource object’s <code>events</code> lists the events the add-on uses;
      the app sends only those, and the rating control names the add-ons that hear a rating. A
      podcast sync that needs only places can list <code>["pause", "progress", "finish"]</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Since revision 9, a <code>start</code> 30 seconds or more into a book carries{' '}
      <code>resumed: true</code>: the listener goes on with a book begun before. Moving a book to
      Currently reading on every <code>start</code> would sign in on every resume, and move a
      finished book back when the listener plays its last minutes again. Since revision 10 the app
      sends it only to an add-on it told about the book before, or for a book marked finished: a
      book begun before the add-on was set up reaches it with its first <code>start</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Events wait on the device and go out when the add-on can be reached, so a train ride offline
      is reported afterwards, with each event’s own <code>at</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Events never carry a source, stream or file URL. Episodes carry their feed and{' '}
      <code>guid</code>; read the public feed if you need more.
    </li>
    <li>
      Answer <code>2xx</code> when the events are handled or deliberately ignored. A{' '}
      <code>4xx</code> (other than <code>408</code> and <code>429</code>) drops them; anything else,
      or no answer, makes the app try again later, waiting longer each time, for up to 30 days. A{' '}
      <code>Retry-After</code> header (seconds or a date, up to a day) makes it wait at least that
      long; the SDK’s <code>AddonError(503, message, {'{ retryAfter }'})</code> sends one. Keep
      handling idempotent: set states rather than adding to them.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h3 id="scrobble-event">ScrobbleEvent</h3>
  <FieldTable fields={scrobbleFields} />
  <h3 id="scrobble-item">ScrobbleItem</h3>
  <FieldTable fields={scrobbleItemFields} />
  <CodeBlock code={scrobbleExample} label="POST /scrobble" />
  <p>
    The SDK validates the body and calls your <code>scrobble</code> handler with the events. See{' '}
    <a href="/docs/scrobbling/">Scrobbling</a> for the first-party Hardcover, gPodder and Open
    Library reading log add-ons.
  </p>
  <h3 id="scrobble-settings">Add-ons inside the app with settings</h3>
  <p>
    Since revision 8, an add-on inside the app with <code>scrobble</code> and <code>settings</code>{' '}
    but no <code>library</code> is set up under Settings → Add-ons, with the form its{' '}
    <code>settings</code> describe. Since revision 9 that holds for every add-on inside the app with{' '}
    <code>settings</code> and no <code>library</code>, and the values reach all of its calls:{' '}
    <code>catalog</code>, <code>meta</code>, <code>stream</code> and <code>artwork</code> get them
    as <code>args.settings</code> too, in the same sandbox, which also reaches the hosts of{' '}
    <code>url</code> fields marked <code>host</code>. Before it is set up, its calls go without
    settings. Addresses in its answers that are off those hosts (a cover, a stream, a link) are left
    out, as for a library’s files: one could carry a setting elsewhere. A library source’s settings
    belong to each server, so its other calls get none. Every value, text fields too, is sealed on
    the phone, and nothing is kept where the phone has no key store; never synced, exported or in
    diagnostics. Instead of <code>POST /scrobble</code>, the app calls <code>{scrobblePath()}</code>{' '}
    in the add-on’s own sandbox, and the handler gets <code>args.events</code> and{' '}
    <code>args.settings</code>. Throw an error with a <code>status</code> to answer it:{' '}
    <code>401</code> when the listener has to set the add-on up again, a <code>5xx</code> to be
    tried later; give the error a <code>retryAfter</code> in seconds to say how long to wait. The
    Open Library reading log, which comes with the app, is one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="versions">Versions</h2>
  <p>
    <code>protocol</code> is the major version, <code>{PROTOCOL_VERSION}</code>. It changes only for
    a breaking change, and the app refuses a manifest whose <code>protocol</code> it does not know.{' '}
    <code>protocolVersion</code> is the revision within it. Revisions only add optional fields and
    routes, so an add-on written for revision 2 still works in an app that only knows revision 1:
    the app ignores what it does not know. A revision 1 add-on, with no <code>protocolVersion</code>
    , works unchanged in every app.
  </p>
  <p>
    Revision 2 adds <code>behaviorHints</code>, a configure flow with sign-in, and{' '}
    <code>/health</code>. Revision 3 adds <a href="#artwork">artwork</a> and{' '}
    <a href="#in-app">add-ons inside the app</a>; revision 4 adds <a href="#badges">badges</a>;
    revision 5 adds <a href="#library">library sources</a>: settings, a library listing and stream
    headers, for add-ons inside the app. An app before revision 5 refuses a manifest that lists{' '}
    <code>library</code>. Revision 6 adds a library’s <code>query</code>, a file’s{' '}
    <code>downloadUrl</code> and <code>place</code>, and <a href="#places">places</a>; an app before
    revision 6 refuses a manifest that lists <code>place</code>. Revision 7 adds a library’s{' '}
    <code>webQuery</code> and <a href="#signin">signing in through a website</a>; an app before
    revision 7 refuses a manifest that lists <code>signin</code>. Revision 8 adds{' '}
    <a href="#scrobble-settings">scrobblers inside the app with settings</a>. Revision 9 adds a
    meta’s <a href="#edition">editions</a>, a <code>start</code>’s <code>resumed</code>, a library’s{' '}
    <a href="#library-pages">pages</a>, and hands the settings of an add-on inside the app without{' '}
    <code>library</code> to <a href="#scrobble-settings">every one of its calls</a>; an app before
    revision 9 ignores them. Revision 10 adds <code>events</code> on the{' '}
    <a href="#scrobble">scrobble</a> resource, sends <code>rate</code> (without <code>rating</code>{' '}
    when taken back), sends <code>resumed</code> only to an add-on that heard of the book, and{' '}
    <code>sessionStartMs</code> on every <code>finish</code> that ends a session; an app before
    revision 10 sends every event to every scrobbler. When an add-on declares a newer revision than
    the app knows, Settings → Add-ons says so. Revision 9 comes with SDK 0.5.0; 0.4.0 covers
    revisions up to 8. Revision 10 comes with the next SDK release, 0.6.
  </p>
  <h3 id="behavior-hints">BehaviorHints</h3>
  <FieldTable fields={behaviorFields} />
  <CodeBlock code={configuredManifest} label="An unconfigured revision 2 manifest" />

  <h2 id="configure">Configuration page</h2>
  <p>
    With <code>behaviorHints.configurable</code>, the app shows a Configure button that opens{' '}
    <code>/configure</code> under the add-on’s base URL in the browser. It is the add-on’s own web
    page: settings, and sign-in when the add-on needs an account with its service. The app never
    sees the form.
  </p>
  <p>
    Add-ons are stateless and the app only remembers the manifest URL, so the page puts the settings
    in a new URL, such as <code>{base}/e1.Q2lw…/manifest.json</code>, and serves every route under
    that prefix. Seal anything private (passwords, tokens) with a key only your add-on has, for
    example AES-GCM, and never store or log it. The app keeps manifest URLs on the device and syncs
    them end-to-end encrypted.
  </p>
  <p>
    The app hands the page a <code>return</code> parameter, and the page sends the URL back:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={configureFlow} label="Configure and sign in" />
  <ul>
    <li>
      Send the listener to{' '}
      <code>
        {'{return}'}?manifest={'{encoded manifest URL}'}
      </code>
      . Accept <code>lelap://addon</code>, the Android app, always. Accept{' '}
      <code>https://{'{origin}'}/addon</code> only for web app origins you list yourself: any other
      address would hand the listener’s settings to a stranger.
    </li>
    <li>
      Without a usable <code>return</code>, show the manifest URL to copy. The listener pastes it
      under Settings → Add-ons, which also accepts the whole <code>lelap://addon?…</code> link.
    </li>
    <li>
      The app matches the new manifest to an installed add-on by <code>id</code>. If one exists, it
      asks to update it, and books, source choices and profiles follow the new URL. Otherwise it
      asks to add it, with the usual trust warning.
    </li>
    <li>
      Opened from a configured URL (<code>{base}/e1.…/configure</code>), the page should show the
      current settings, never a password.
    </li>
    <li>
      Say who runs the page, and never pass it off as Lelap’s: it asks for tokens and passwords, and
      a page that looks like the app’s own would make its trust warning meaningless. Pages the SDK
      serves end with “This page is run by {'{host}'}, not by Lelap.”
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The <a href="/docs/sdk/#configure">SDK</a> does all of this from a list of fields.
  </p>

  <h2 id="health">Health</h2>
  <p>
    Settings → Add-ons shows each add-on’s last response time and last error, from the requests the
    app makes anyway. It stays on the device. An add-on with <code>behaviorHints.health</code> also
    answers <code>GET /health</code>, which the app asks when Settings opens and when the listener
    taps Check now. Answer quickly, and with <code>200</code> even when the status is{' '}
    <code>down</code>.
  </p>
  <FieldTable fields={healthFields} />
  <CodeBlock code={healthExample} label="GET /health" />

  <h2 id="artwork">Artwork</h2>
  <p>
    Since revision 3, an add-on with the <code>artwork</code> resource answers{' '}
    <code>
      GET /artwork/{'{type}'}/{'{id}'}.json
    </code>{' '}
    with pictures for an item it knows, as{' '}
    <code>{'{ "artwork": [{ "url", "kind", "width", "height" }] }'}</code>. The app asks it only for
    items that come without a cover, picks the best square cover (at least 300 px when one is), and
    draws any <a href="#badges">badges</a> itself: send plain pictures, not covers with text baked
    in. <code>kind</code> is <code>cover</code> today; other kinds are kept and ignored.
  </p>

  <h2 id="badges">Badges</h2>
  <p>
    Since revision 4, a <code>MetaPreview</code> or <code>Meta</code> can carry <code>badges</code>:
    a rating and a count of new episodes, as data. The app draws them on the cover in its own style,
    next to its own badges for what the listener finished or how far in they are, and reads them out
    as part of the item’s name. Listeners can turn badges off in Settings. Badges travel with
    catalog items, so a row of covers needs no extra request; the artwork route stays for pictures
    only.
  </p>
  <p>
    An app from before revision 4 ignores <code>badges</code>, and an add-on without them works as
    before. Fields the app does not know are ignored, so later revisions can add more. Badges that
    do not fit, such as a rating of 7, are dropped and the item still shows.
  </p>
  <FieldTable fields={badgeFields} />

  <h2 id="permissions">What the listener sees before installing</h2>
  <p>
    Before an add-on is installed, the app lists what its manifest says it does, in plain words:
    which catalogs it shows, that it looks up details, finds audio, supplies covers, or receives
    what the listener plays (<code>scrobble</code>, shown in amber). It also flags an address that
    is not encrypted, or that holds settings, and names the host the app talks to. Declare only the
    resources you serve.
  </p>

  <h2 id="in-app">Add-ons inside the app</h2>
  <p>
    Since revision 3, an add-on can also be a single JavaScript file that runs inside the app, for
    sources on the listener's own network that have no server for an add-on: a NAS, a shelf of files
    behind a small web server. The listener pastes the file's address in Settings → Add-ons; the app
    keeps the code on the device and shows its fingerprint before installing.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The file calls <code>addon({'{ manifest, catalog, meta, stream, artwork }'})</code>. Handlers
      get <code>{'{ type, id, extra }'}</code> and return the same JSON a web add-on answers;{' '}
      <code>null</code> means not found.
    </li>
    <li>
      The manifest declares <code>sandbox.hosts</code>, such as <code>192.168.1.20:8096</code>,{' '}
      <code>nas.local</code> or <code>*.home.arpa</code>; a host without a port is reached over
      https only, so write <code>nas.local:80</code> for plain http. The code runs with no network
      of its own; <code>lelap.fetch(url, init)</code> asks the app, which fetches only from those
      hosts, without the app's cookies and without following redirects (a redirect is an error), and
      refuses everything else.
    </li>
    <li>
      Limits: 20 seconds per call and per fetch, 5 MB per answer, 1 MB of code. Audio URLs it
      returns are played directly, like any stream.
    </li>
    <li>
      In the web app, a browser blocks plain <code>http</code> requests from an <code>https</code>{' '}
      page and needs CORS headers, so in-app add-ons are mostly for the Android app.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a href={`${site.repoUrl}/blob/main/examples/in-app-addon/home-shelf.js`}>
      examples/in-app-addon/home-shelf.js
    </a>{' '}
    reads a <code>books.json</code> from a server at home.
  </p>

  <h2 id="library">Library sources</h2>
  <p>
    Since revision 5, an add-on inside the app can be a source for the listener’s own library, next
    to WebDAV and S3: it lists the books and files on a server, and the app scans them into the
    library with places, downloads and offline play. The listener adds it in Settings → Servers →
    Add a server, where it shows as a kind of its own and asks for what <code>settings</code>{' '}
    describes. Lelap ships four with the app: HTTP folder, which reads the folder listings of
    servers such as nginx, Apache and Caddy; Subsonic, for Navidrome, Airsonic-Advanced, Gonic and
    other servers that speak the Subsonic API; Emby; and Plex.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>settings</code> in the manifest describes the form. The values stay on the phone,
      passwords sealed like a WebDAV password, never synced, exported or in diagnostics. They reach
      only this add-on’s <code>library</code> handler, as <code>args.settings</code>, for that
      server. Put keys and tokens in <code>password</code> fields: text and address fields are kept
      like a server’s address, unsealed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>{`GET /${libraryPath()}`}</code> answers every file, with a <code>headers</code> object
      when the audio needs a login. Libraries are scanned when the app starts and when the listener
      scans, with the other servers.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each server runs in a sandbox of its own that reaches only <code>sandbox.hosts</code> and the
      hosts of <code>url</code> fields marked <code>host</code>, with their port. The code runs in a
      worker inside it, which can’t open a page or an address and has no <code>fetch</code> of its
      own; where a worker can’t start, the add-on gets no settings.
    </li>
    <li>
      The app sends the headers with the audio on the phone (playing, downloading, casting through
      the phone) only to those hosts, drops them when a redirect leaves the server, and leaves out
      any file or cover elsewhere: an address could otherwise carry a setting off.
    </li>
    <li>
      A browser can’t send headers with audio, so books that need them play in the Android app,
      unless the listing also gives a{' '}
      <a href="#web-query">
        <code>webQuery</code>
      </a>
      .
    </li>
  </ul>
  <CodeBlock code={sourceManifest} label="manifest" />
  <h3 id="setting-field">SettingField</h3>
  <FieldTable fields={settingFields} />
  <CodeBlock code={library} label={`GET /${libraryPath()}`} />
  <h3 id="library-file">LibraryFile</h3>
  <FieldTable fields={libraryFields} />
  <p>
    <code>title</code> and <code>artist</code> work as in a file’s tags, and <code>chapters</code>{' '}
    is a list of <a href="#chapter">Chapter</a>. The SDK’s <code>readSettings</code> checks a form
    the same way the app does, and <code>settingHosts</code> gives the hosts it grants.
  </p>

  <h3 id="library-pages">Pages</h3>
  <p>
    Since revision 9, a large library can come in pages. An answer is at most 5 MB, about 20,000
    files; give <code>next</code>, any string, when there are more, and the app calls{' '}
    <code>library</code> again with it as <code>args.library.cursor</code>, joining the files, up to
    1,000 pages. <code>headers</code>, <code>query</code> and <code>webQuery</code> come from the
    first page. An app before revision 9 keeps the first page only. Once the listing is done, the
    app closes the server’s sandbox until it is needed again.
  </p>

  <h3 id="query">A login in the address</h3>
  <p>
    Since revision 6, a listing can carry <code>query</code> instead of <code>headers</code>, for
    servers that only take a login in the address, such as the Subsonic API. The app keeps it sealed
    with the settings and adds it to a file’s address only at the moment it plays, downloads or
    reads the file, and only on the add-on’s hosts. The addresses it stores, syncs, exports or logs
    never have it. A browser can play these books too, since the login is in the address. A file’s{' '}
    <code>cover</code> is shown as it is, without <code>query</code>; put the cover in the listing
    as the folder’s <code>cover.jpg</code> instead when it needs the login.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={subsonicLibrary} label={`GET /${libraryPath()}, from a Subsonic server`} />

  <h3 id="places">Places</h3>
  <p>
    Since revision 6, a library source can keep the listener’s places on its server, both ways. A
    file’s <code>place</code> brings the server’s place into the library on each scan; an add-on
    with the <code>place</code> resource also gets a <code>PlaceReport</code> in its{' '}
    <code>place</code> handler, as <code>args.place</code> next to <code>args.settings</code>, when
    the listener starts, pauses, plays on for about five minutes or finishes a book. The newest
    place wins. Only the profile that added the server sends places, and a report that fails is not
    sent again: the next scan settles it. There is no HTTP route for <code>{placePath()}</code>;
    only the sandbox calls it.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={placeReport} label="PlaceReport" />

  <h3 id="web-query">Headers in the browser</h3>
  <p>
    Since revision 7, a listing with <code>headers</code> can also carry <code>webQuery</code>:
    where the app can’t send headers, as with a browser’s audio element, it adds these query
    parameters to a file’s address instead, sealed and only on the add-on’s hosts, as with{' '}
    <code>query</code>. On the phone the headers go as before and <code>webQuery</code> is never
    used. Emby and Plex send their token this way.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={plexLibrary} label={`GET /${libraryPath()}, from a Plex server`} />

  <h3 id="signin">Signing in through a website</h3>
  <p>
    Since revision 7, an add-on can sign the listener in on a website instead of asking for a
    password, as Plex does with a code entered at plex.tv/link. It lists the <code>signin</code>{' '}
    resource and has one setting of type <code>signin</code>; the form shows a Sign in button for
    it.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The button calls <code>{signinPath('start')}</code>. The add-on answers a{' '}
      <code>SigninStart</code>: a <code>code</code> and an https <code>url</code>, which the app
      shows with a button to open it, on this device or another.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every <code>interval</code> seconds until <code>expiresAt</code> the app calls{' '}
      <code>{signinPath('check')}</code>, with <code>args.signin.state</code> as the start gave it.
      An answer without <code>settings</code> means the listener isn’t done yet.
    </li>
    <li>
      An answer with <code>settings</code> fills in the form by field key: the <code>signin</code>{' '}
      field’s value is whatever the add-on keeps (a token), and other fields may be filled too. An
      address field marked <code>host</code> is filled only for add-ons that come with the app, such
      as Plex with the server’s addresses; for any other add-on the listener types the address, so
      it can’t choose its own hosts. When the listener logs in again to a server already added, only
      the sealed fields change.
    </li>
    <li>
      Both calls get <code>args.settings</code> with the form so far, in the server’s own sandbox,
      reaching only <code>sandbox.hosts</code> (for Plex, <code>plex.tv:443</code>). The page in{' '}
      <code>url</code> must be on one of those hosts too. There is no HTTP route for them; only the
      sandbox calls them.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <CodeBlock code={signinStart} label={signinPath('start')} />
  <CodeBlock code={signinResult} label={signinPath('check')} />

  <h2 id="speed">Making it fast</h2>
  <p>
    Listeners feel every add-on in Home, Discover and Search. The app shows each add-on's answer as
    soon as it arrives and gives up on a request after 10 seconds, but how quickly results appear is
    up to the add-on:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Answer in well under a second.</strong> Search runs on every few keystrokes; aim for a
      few hundred milliseconds, and return fewer results rather than slower ones.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cache what you fetch.</strong> Keep answers from the services you call (Cache API or
      KV on Workers, memory or Redis elsewhere), and search results for popular queries for a few
      minutes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Say how long an answer stays good:</strong> <code>cacheMaxAge</code> in the JSON and a
      matching <code>Cache-Control</code> header, long for metadata that rarely changes, short for
      new and trending lists.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keep lists small:</strong> previews only (title, author, cover), around 20–50 per
      page, with <code>skip</code> for more; full details belong in <code>meta</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Don't fan out per item:</strong> one upstream call per catalog page, not one per book.
      Pre-build lists that don't depend on the listener.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fail fast:</strong> if a service you depend on is down, answer with what you have or
      an error at once instead of waiting for your own timeouts; report it in{' '}
      <a href="#health">/health</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Serve covers small:</strong> square, around 300–600 px, from a CDN.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="schema">JSON Schema</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/docs/protocol.schema.json">
      <code>/docs/protocol.schema.json</code>
    </a>{' '}
    is a JSON Schema (draft 2020-12) generated from the SDK’s schemas, with <code>Manifest</code>,{' '}
    <code>CatalogResponse</code>, <code>MetaResponse</code>, <code>StreamResponse</code>,{' '}
    <code>ScrobbleRequest</code>, <code>HealthResponse</code> and <code>LibraryResponse</code> under{' '}
    <code>$defs</code>. Point a validator or your editor at one of them:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={schemaRef} />
</Docs>
